1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,690
Why is the sky blue?

2
00:00:02,347 --> 00:00:03,737
Sunlight looks white.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,170
But white light is every

4
00:00:06,170 --> 00:00:07,273
color at once.

5
00:00:07,900 --> 00:00:09,400
Red, orange, yellow,

6
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:11,140
green, blue and violet all

7
00:00:11,140 --> 00:00:12,230
travel together.

8
00:00:13,033 --> 00:00:14,813
Each color is a wave,

9
00:00:14,813 --> 00:00:16,043
and every wave has its

10
00:00:16,043 --> 00:00:16,928
own length.

11
00:00:17,533 --> 00:00:19,493
Red waves are long and lazy.

12
00:00:19,783 --> 00:00:21,663
Blue waves are short and tight.

13
00:00:22,533 --> 00:00:23,293
In the air,

14
00:00:23,463 --> 00:00:25,513
sunlight meets tiny molecules of

15
00:00:25,513 --> 00:00:27,263
nitrogen and oxygen.

16
00:00:27,933 --> 00:00:29,133
They bounce the light away

17
00:00:29,133 --> 00:00:30,463
in every direction.

18
00:00:31,353 --> 00:00:32,833
Short blue waves scatter far

19
00:00:32,903 --> 00:00:35,267
more strongly than long red ones.

20
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:37,360
So blue light reaches you

21
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:39,020
from every part of the sky.

22
00:00:40,033 --> 00:00:41,303
Look up, and that scattered

23
00:00:41,303 --> 00:00:42,703
blue is what you see.

24
00:00:43,546 --> 00:00:45,376
At sunset, light crosses much

25
00:00:45,376 --> 00:00:46,776
more air, and the blue

26
00:00:46,776 --> 00:00:47,976
is scattered away.

27
00:00:48,843 --> 00:00:50,543
Lord Rayleigh explained it in

28
00:00:50,543 --> 00:00:53,743
1871: the air scatters blue light.
